---
import data from "../data/site-data.json";
import Figure from "./Figure.astro";
import { gh, int, num } from "./format";

const n = data.nlsy;
const cal = Object.fromEntries(n.calibration.map((c) => [c.range_pct, c]));
const models = Object.fromEntries(n.heldOut.models.map((m) => [m.id, m]));
---
<section class="section" id="expectations" aria-labelledby="ch1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="grid chapter-head">
      <div class="ch-meta c-1-4"><span class="ch-num">Chapter 1</span><span class="ch-code">M01–M08 · NLSY97</span></div>
      <div class="c-4-13">
        <h2 class="ch-title" id="ch1">Stated expectations, and the births that followed</h2>
        <p class="standfirst">
          Before asking what Generation Z intends, the project asked how much stated plans are worth. In 2009–10, {int(n.sample.labelled)} women in
          their late twenties answered a direct question: what is the percent chance that you will have a child in the next five years? Their later
          birth histories show what happened.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <dl class="strip">
      <div><dt>Population</dt><dd>Women born 1980–84, NLSY97 cohort</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Baseline</dt><dd>Interview in 2009–10, ages {n.sample.age.min}–{n.sample.age.max}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Outcome</dt><dd>At least one birth in the next five years</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Weighting</dt><dd>Unweighted sample, {int(n.sample.labelled)} women</dd></div>
      <div class="not"><dt>Not</dt><dd>Generation Z, and not a population estimate</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="c-1-9">
        <Figure
          num="1.1"
          anchor="fig-calibration"
          title="Stated chances were informative, but far too extreme"
          sub="Share of women who had a birth within five years, by the chance they stated at baseline. 3,404 women with a known outcome."
          chart="calibration"
          fallback="stated_expectation_calibration.png"
          fallbackAlt="Calibration plot: observed birth rates sit above the diagonal for low stated chances and well below it for high stated chances."
          minH={420}
        >
          <Fragment slot="controls">
            <button class="toggle" type="button" data-toggle="dist" aria-pressed="false">How women answered</button>
            <button class="toggle" type="button" data-toggle="recal" aria-pressed="false">Fitted correction</button>
          </Fragment>
          <p><span class="k">Uncertainty</span>95% Wilson intervals for each range. Hollow circles: fewer than 50 women.</p>
          <p><span class="k">Source</span>NLSY97 public-use data (BLS); <a href={gh("data/public/dashboard_summary.json")}>dashboard_summary.json</a>.</p>
          <p class="wide cannot"><span class="k">Cannot show</span>How Generation Z's answers would behave. This is one earlier cohort, unweighted; about 5% of the women were already pregnant when they answered.</p>
          <details class="data-table">
            <summary>Data table</summary>
            <div class="table-scroll">
              <table class="data">
                <thead><tr><th>Stated range</th><th class="n">Women</th><th class="n">Births</th><th class="n">Mean stated</th><th class="n">Observed</th><th class="n">95% interval</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  {n.calibration.map((c) => (
                    <tr><td>{c.range_pct}%</td><td class="n">{int(c.n)}</td><td class="n">{int(c.births)}</td><td class="n">{num(c.mean_stated_pct)}%</td><td class="n">{num(c.observed_pct)}%</td><td class="n">{num(c.ci_low_pct)}–{num(c.ci_high_pct)}%</td></tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </details>
        </Figure>
      </div>
      <aside class="c-10-13" style="padding-top:var(--s-96)">
        <div class="aside">
          <span class="label">Reading the chart</span>
          If stated chances were perfectly calibrated, every circle would sit on the diagonal: of women who said 30%, about 30% would have had a birth.
          Circles above the line mean more births than people expected; below the line, fewer.
        </div>
        <div class="aside">
          <span class="label">Calibration slope</span>
          A single number for how stretched the answers were: {num(n.slope.value, 2)} (95% interval {num(n.slope.ci[0], 2)}–{num(n.slope.ci[1], 2)}), where 1 would be perfect.
          Below 1 means the answers were more extreme than the outcomes.
        </div>
        <div class="aside">
          <span class="label">Ranking</span>
          The answers still ranked women well: a woman who had a birth usually gave a higher number than one who did not (AUC {num(n.forecast.auc, 3)}).
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="prose c-1-8">
        <p>
          Women who said there was no chance of a birth were not right about that: <strong>{num(cal["0"].observed_pct)}%</strong> of them had one within five years
          ({int(cal["0"].n)} women; 95% interval {num(cal["0"].ci_low_pct)}–{num(cal["0"].ci_high_pct)}%). Women who said they were certain were not right either:
          <strong>{num(cal["100"].observed_pct)}%</strong> had a birth ({int(cal["100"].n)} women; {num(cal["100"].ci_low_pct)}–{num(cal["100"].ci_high_pct)}%).
        </p>
        <p>
          The answers also cluster: {num(n.distribution.share_pct.at_0_50_or_100)}% of women gave exactly 0, 50 or 100. They behave partly like categories
          (no, maybe, yes) rather than finely graded probabilities. The finding held under every alternative outcome rule and every bound for the
          {int(n.sample.unknown)} women whose outcome is unknown.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="c-1-9">
        <Figure
          num="1.2"
          anchor="fig-brier"
          title="Correcting the stated answers made better forecasts"
          sub={`Forecast error for four approaches on ${int(n.heldOut.n)} women from households that were not used to fit them (${int(n.heldOut.births)} births).`}
          chart="brier"
          fallback="baseline_comparison.png"
          fallbackAlt="Dot plots of Brier score and ROC AUC for four approaches on held-out households."
          minH={260}
        >
          <p><span class="k">Uncertainty</span>95% household-cluster bootstrap intervals, 2,000 replicates.</p>
          <p><span class="k">Reading it</span>The Brier score is the average squared gap between forecast and outcome: 0 is perfect, and a constant rate scores {num(models.A.brier, 3)} here.</p>
          <p class="wide cannot"><span class="k">Cannot show</span>Whether the same correction would work for another cohort. A single held-out split; repeated cross-validation found the gain from background characteristics consistent but modest.</p>
        </Figure>
      </div>
      <aside class="c-10-13" style="padding-top:var(--s-96)">
        <div class="aside">
          <span class="label">What changed</span>
          Used as given, the stated chance forecast worse than a constant rate ({num(models.B.brier, 3)} against {num(models.A.brier, 3)}), because the answers were too extreme.
          A simple logistic correction kept their ranking and fixed their scale ({num(models.C.brier, 3)}). Adding background characteristics helped a little more ({num(models.D.brier, 3)}).
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <div class="limits">
      <span class="label">Limits of this chapter</span>
      <ul>
        <li>An earlier cohort, born 1980–84. Whether Generation Z's stated expectations behave the same way is untested.</li>
        <li>Five-year expectations, not lifetime desires or completed family size.</li>
        <li>Unweighted sample results, not estimates for the U.S. population.</li>
        <li>{int(n.sample.unknown)} women with unknown outcomes are excluded; bounds for them do not change the finding.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="further">
      <a href={gh("docs/methodology.md")}>Chapter methodology</a>
      <a href="#archive-figures">Original figures R1.1–R1.3</a>
      <a href={gh("data/public/dashboard_summary.json")}>Public data</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
